No conforming Judgment Pack can implement this policy on every described input.

The blocker is the policy’s treatment of omitted `newVendor`, `criticalSupplier`, and `priorEnforcement` as `"no"`. Under JPS §7.4, an omitted fact path evaluates to `unknown`; Core provides no presence test, default-value operator, or way to distinguish omission from another unknown fact result. Consequently, a condition cannot implement “equals no or is omitted.”

For example, O2 must force `review` when `criticalSupplier` is `"yes"` but behave exactly as `"no"` when the member is omitted. An exception can force review for `"yes"` with `onUnknown: ignore`, but ordinary rules involving the field cannot establish the required omitted-as-no branch. Similar non-representability affects O1 and D5, especially where their precedence must interact with unreadable risk, spend, or country under U1.

Because the requested behavior is not expressible in the supplied language, any pack block would either fail hidden policy cases or misrepresent itself as a complete implementation. A valid solution requires either:

- inputs normalized to report all three statuses explicitly as `"yes"` or `"no"`; or
- a JPS extension/operator for fact presence or defaulting, which the task forbids by requiring no extensions.
